def p(t,k='body'): blocks.append(dict(kind=k,text=t))
def page(t): p('','page');p(t,'h1')
p('TV programme recommendation','title');p('Midterm | Gleb Alektorov | 24 October 2026','h2')
p('Predicting five preferred programmes from viewing intervals and the broadcast schedule. Implementation, chronological evaluation and platform strategy.')
p('Repository: https://github.com/Moonkato/llm4rec_midterm/tree/main/solutions/midterm')
p('The implementation and final CSV are packaged for this repository; remote publication is performed by the repository owner. Code, evidence and candidate CSVs are stored in solutions/midterm.')
p('1. Task and data','h1')
p('The output is exactly five ordered, distinct, nonzero tv_show_id values for each of 1,260 template users. The forecast covers weeks 31-42 (27 July to 19 October 2020). MAP@5 rewards relevant programmes near the top of the list. User IDs are preserved as strings to avoid 64-bit precision loss.')
p('The supplied data contains 5,821,987 viewing intervals and three EPG schedule files: 1,074,235, 1,074,394 and 1,295,378 rows. A viewing duration is session length, not programme completion. The median raw session lasts 396 seconds, while extreme sessions can span days. Only 10.8% of schedule rows in weeks 11-20 carry a programme ID; this percentage describes rows, not duration-weighted airtime coverage.')
p('A session [s,e) is matched to an airing [a,b) only on the same channel, with overlap max(0, min(e,b)-max(s,a)). Airing duration D=b-a provides the denominator. All historical features stop at the relevant cutoff. Interval timestamps use the repository clock conversion, including its March 29 one-hour adjustment; replay checks cover missing-hour and midnight cases.')
p('Initially, an airing was watched if merged overlapping fragments covered at least 180 seconds. This filters brief channel switches relative to the observed session median, but does not account for programme length. The later completion-oriented protocol uses more than 30% of an airing for history and more than 80% for target counts, using a single overlapping fragment rather than the union of interrupted fragments. The legacy target takes the maximum stored overlap fragment; fragments separated by the artificial May 18 preprocessing boundary are not rejoined. Replay features instead use raw continuous sessions. This is an operational approximation to preferred programmes, not a verified reproduction of the hidden Kaggle label generator.')
p('ID 0 is excluded from every item list and target. Its sessions can still describe channel/time viewing context. Future EPG supplies programme availability, channel, start time and duration only; no test-period views enter training or features. Metadata for the content model comes from the historical scope.')
p('2. Evaluation contract','h1')
p('The main holdout is 18 May-27 July 2020; histories end on 18 May. The later robustness window is 22 June-27 July, with history ending on 22 June. These windows overlap and are not independent test sets. Supervised rankers use an earlier fit task: history 9 March-13 April and labels 13 April-18 May. Repeated validation-guided experimentation creates selection optimism; the later window reduces but does not eliminate it.')
p('For user u, AP@5 = sum over ranks r=1..5 of Precision@r times relevance(r), divided by min(5, number of relevant items). Users with empty truth receive zero. MAP@5 averages all 1,260 users. The initial target is all distinct qualifying 180-second items; the later target selects the five most frequent qualifying >80% airings, with numeric-ID tie breaking. These are different metrics even though both are called MAP@5.')
page('3. Implemented methods and common output rules')
p('Every system permits repeats of previously watched programmes and restricts output to nonzero programmes airing during the forecast window. Cold users receive historical unique-viewer popularity restricted to eligible items. Zero-signal or short lists are completed by that common fallback without duplicates. Ties use ascending numeric programme ID; final rankings are truncated to five. The fallback rule is shared, but the number of cold users can change with the historical qualification threshold.')
p('Content-based filtering','h2')
p('Historical item attributes are tokenised into category, genre, director, actor and year/decade blocks. IDF is log((1+N)/(1+df))+1. Each block is L2-normalised and weighted by 1.0, 1.5, 0.75, 1.0 and 0.35 respectively; the concatenated item vector is normalised again. A user profile is the normalised sum of item vectors weighted by log(1+historical airing count). Cosine similarity produces a ranking, not a viewing probability. Missing metadata gives a zero vector; there are no title or identity tokens. The observed vocabulary has 11,939 features across the five blocks.')
p('Implicit-feedback matrix factorization','h2')
p('ALS minimises sum c_ui(p_ui-x_u dot y_i)^2 plus lambda times the squared factor norms, with p_ui=1 for a positive count and c_ui=1+alpha*log(1+count_ui). Missing pairs have preference zero and confidence one; they are not treated as explicit dislikes. Alternating normal equations are solved in float64, and stored factors use float32. Dot products rank eligible programmes.')
p('The historical matrix contains 4,851 users, 8,233 items and 651,903 nonzeros. Tested (factors, alpha) pairs were (32,1), (32,10), (64,10), (64,40); regularization=1, iterations=10, seed=20261022. The best initial ALS variant was 32 factors, alpha=1. No validation labels enter factor fitting.')
p('Popularity and personal frequency','h2')
p('Popularity ranks by distinct historical viewers. Personal frequency ranks each user\'s historical qualifying airing counts and uses popularity to fill zero-signal slots. Popularity is a required baseline and personal frequency is an additional heuristic; neither is counted as one of the two required methods. Content filtering and implicit ALS satisfy that requirement.')
p('Initial controlled comparison','h2')
p('All rows below use the same 180-second histories, holdout, all-item truth, eligible catalogue, repeat policy and fallback. Fit times exclude data preprocessing. These high all-item scores must not be interpreted as competition scores.')
for r in d['comparison_180s']: p(f"{r['method']}: MAP@5 {r['map5']:.6f}; fit {r['fit_seconds']:.3f} s; ranking/evaluation {r['rank_evaluation_seconds']:.3f} s.")
p('The complete initial round took 23.22 seconds and peaked at 385 MB RSS on macOS arm64, Python 3.13.1, NumPy 2.5.4 and SciPy 1.18.1, using one compute thread. ALS variants with larger confidence or dimension performed worse here; increasing capacity was not evidence of better generalization.')
page('4. Schedule-aware ranking and final strategy')
p('The weekly ranker v3 combines 19 history/popularity/channel/hour/availability features with five weekly-context features: expected minutes, expected airing fraction, recent-28-day minutes, activity frequency and a recent/all-history ratio. Candidate retrieval combines personal candidates (up to 100), popularity (30) and channel-hour candidates (30), then deduplicates. This shortlist is a system component, not the entire eligible EPG catalogue.')
p('LightGBM LambdaRank uses 417 trees, 15 leaves, maximum depth 4, minimum child count 100, L2=10, learning rate 0.03, label gains [0,1], truncation 10 and seed 20261023. The configuration and 417-tree count were selected from three configurations on earlier inner-fit/inner-stop temporal tasks with early-stopping patience 100, then refitted on the April 13-May 18 task. Training retains queries with both relevant and nonrelevant candidates. It learns ordering against the top-five completion-count target. All fitted feature transforms and the model are frozen before each evaluation forecast.')
p('The airing model scores individual candidate airings from 33 features: the history/context base plus channel, weekday, hour, duration, availability and session-coverage features. A binary LightGBM with 300 trees, 15 leaves, learning rate 0.05, minimum leaf count 100, L2=10 and max_bin=63 and seed=20261023 is trained on 473,908 sampled rows (102,907 positive; 371,001 negative). Negative sampling probability is 0.01, with inverse-sampling weights 100 for negatives and 1 for positives. Summed per-airing predictions form an expected-count ranking; they are not calibrated satisfaction probabilities.')
p('Hybrid v4 combines v3 and the airing model with equal reciprocal-rank fusion: score(i)=0.5/(60+rank_v3(i))+0.5/(60+rank_event(i)). Rank fusion avoids pretending that a LambdaRank score and an expected count share a probability scale. Equal weights and the RRF constant 60 were prespecified in the airing-model protocol, rather than optimized on the final holdout. This fixed fusion improved both chronological windows relative to either component. It was the strongest candidate among the first four submissions; the fifth submission subsequently improved both returned scores.')
p('Matched-pool system comparison','h2')
p(d['strict_top5_rescoring']['note'])
for name,score in d['strict_top5_rescoring']['scores'].items(): p(f'{name}: strict top-five-count MAP@5 = {score:.6f}.','small')
p('These matched-pool results supersede the earlier mixed-retrieval table for comparing systems. The original unmodified v4 validation remains 0.417238 (main) and 0.365450 (later); the small main-window change here is solely the standardized fallback. No Kaggle score is attributed to this audit wrapper. The initial 180-second table remains the controlled model-family comparison with identical historical labels.')
p('Robustness window: v3 0.361875; airing 0.356944; v4 0.365450. Main-window v4 improves on v3 by 0.009825 and on the airing model by 0.004984. Reusing the same holdout for many experiments limits the certainty of these gains.')
page('5. Final experiment, submission evidence and limitations')
p('The final experiment added categorical user/programme IDs to the original 24-feature ranker (26 features), and exact historical same-weekday/channel session-replay features to the original 33-feature airing model (37 features). Encoders were fitted only on training rows; unknown categories receive -1. Replay features measure >30% and >80% single-session overlap, a recent-28-day completion rate and observable historical-date count. Sessions are clipped at the cutoff; interrupted fragments are never merged into a synthetic continuous session.')
p('Two fits completed in 791.59 seconds with 1.026 GB peak RSS. The combined identity/replay arm reached 0.419222 on the main holdout and 0.364225 on the later window, versus v4 at 0.417238 and 0.365450. Main delta +0.001984 has paired 95% interval [-0.001883, 0.005796]; later delta -0.001225 has interval [-0.005427, 0.002919]. It failed the prespecified consistency screen. The combined CSV was submitted for the final external check. Kaggle returned Private 0.44559 and Public 0.45546, both higher than v4. The screenshot below confirms the outcome; it does not erase the earlier mixed validation evidence.')
p('Earlier attempts included expanded weekly candidates, EASE features, rolling and full-history training, recurrence features, alternative objectives and user-fold out-of-fold stacking. None established a clear, consistent replacement for v4. LightFM installation failed under Python 3.13 and no LightFM training result is claimed. No external or synthetic viewing records were added. Stopping avoids spending more compute on small, uncertain offline gains.')
