page('7. Platform choice and interpretation')
p('I designate v5 (identity ranker plus replay airing model, equal RRF60) as the final competition strategy because the completed external check improved both Private and Public MAP@5 over v4. V4 remains the stronger reference on the later local window. For a platform, v5 is the candidate I would take forward to an online evaluation, with popularity fallback retained; the small offline gains and mixed local evidence do not establish an online benefit or statistical superiority on hidden labels.')
p('For user 15634861151839696048, the historical profile includes news programmes such as Facts and TSN. Popularity achieves AP@5=0.40, ALS 0.55, the weekly ranker 0.76 and v4 0.80. The schedule-aware system largely recovers recurring preferences. This is useful familiarity, not evidence of discovery or enjoyment. Content filtering scores zero despite descriptive attributes: semantic resemblance alone does not recover this user\'s five most frequent future programmes.')
p('For user 10269329346053634491, more history does not make the prediction easy. Popularity reaches 0.18, content and ALS zero, personal frequency 0.10, and v4 0.20. The actual top five contain programmes missed by the models. The contrast supports a failure case involving changing or insufficiently represented preferences; it does not establish that all heavy viewers are difficult. The complete ordered IDs and observed hits above make this judgment inspectable.')
p('ALS shares statistical strength through low-rank co-preference patterns, but this task rewards repeated schedules and strongly time-dependent availability. Content features can connect related metadata where co-view data is thin, yet missing or generic attributes and absent historical vectors for new items limit that benefit here. Association rules would express direct conditional co-occurrence; graph diffusion would spread evidence through multiple co-view links. Those mechanisms differ from ALS, but no unreported association/PPR score is used to justify the final choice.')
p('Popularity already captures broadly watched news and is extremely cheap. It cannot express the user\'s programme-specific weekday routine. The hybrid costs more, particularly when evaluating millions of airings, but runs at this scale within about 1 GB and minutes rather than requiring a GPU. Candidate caching and collapsed repeated airing patterns reduce inference cost. V5 export took 399.66 seconds, peaked at 838 MB and scored 8,297,845 replay tuples without new fits.')
p('Coverage is the primary limitation: most EPG rows lack item IDs, so both learning and evaluation ignore much of what people watch. Non-viewed programmes are not proven dislikes, long sessions may be passive background viewing, and device/channel activity does not reveal enjoyment. Completion counts overweight repeated short programmes and changing broadcast supply. Only three test users are cold under the final export protocol, but sparse-history and new-programme weaknesses remain.')
p('No live A/B test, revenue lift, retention effect or causal business outcome was measured. An operational decision should preserve repeat recommendations where supported by behaviour and evaluate discovery separately; unfamiliar output is not automatically a better recommendation. The available evidence supports choosing the hybrid for this dataset, not a universal ranking of recommendation families.')
page('8. Verification, reproducibility and assistance')
p('The final audit reran 16 independent hand-calculated AP@5 fixtures: all passed. Re-evaluation of stored scores reproduced the reported original metrics. Both v4 and v5 CSVs were independently checked against the template, eligible catalogue and saved fusion scores: all 1,260 ranked lists match, with five distinct nonzero IDs each. Source review found no future-viewing feature leakage in the examined builders. This was a source/artifact audit, not a fresh full raw-data rebuild or proof that every possible defect is absent.')
p('A shared-candidate/shared-fallback audit closed the comparison discrepancy between earlier baselines and advanced systems. The expanded ten-method audit took 3.02 seconds without fitting. Reproduction: solutions/midterm/.venv/bin/python solutions/midterm/tools/build_matched_pool_comparison.py. Evidence: evidence/matched_pool_comparison.json, audit_code_final.json and audit_session_final.json.')

p('Implemented sources: models/content.py and models/implicit_als.py; tools/build_interactions.py and run_round1.py; tools/run_deep_temporal.py, run_airing_model.py and export_airing_fusion.py; tools/run_final_identity.py, run_final_replay.py and export_final_factorial.py. Evidence is stored in per-experiment JSON files with protocols, metrics, runtimes and hashes. tools/build_report_comparison.py re-evaluates saved predictions without training. Tools/build_report.py and render_report.swift regenerate this document from evidence.')
p('Run from the repository root using solutions/midterm/.venv/bin/python. The saved environment is Python 3.13 on Apple arm64 (Python 3.13.1, NumPy 2.5.4, SciPy 1.18.1, LightGBM 4.7.0); seeds and thread limits are fixed in the relevant scripts. Raw data, caches and local checkpoints are required for full reproduction and are not implied to be committed. No fresh full end-to-end rerun was performed while writing this report.')
p('AI assistance was used for implementation, debugging, experiment design, interpretation and report preparation. The supplied competition data is the empirical source; no external viewing dataset or pretrained recommender was used. External methodological guidance included LightGBM categorical-feature documentation and the published two-layer cinema recommender solution. Its reported score was not treated as a target guarantee.')
